{"causes":["The publication of Jules Verne's 'Around the World in Eighty Days' was influenced by the scientific discoveries of the time, including Charles Darwin's theory of evolution and the development of the telegraph and steamships.","The novel's themes of global connectivity and technological innovation were shaped by the growing importance of international trade and communication during the Industrial Revolution.","The character of Phileas Fogg was inspired by real-life figures such as the famous American adventurer and explorer, John Wesley Powell."],"effects":["The novel helped to popularize the concept of 'round-the-world travel' among the general public, paving the way for modern-day tourism and adventure travel.","Around the World in Eighty Days contributed to the development of science fiction as a literary genre, influencing generations of writers and thinkers.","The novel's portrayal of global connectivity and technological innovation helped to shape public perceptions of international relations and diplomacy during the late 19th century."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"jules-verne","sourceName":"Jules Verne","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"around-the-world-in-eighty-days","targetName":"Around the World in Eighty Days","context":"Verne's groundbreaking science fiction novel that captured the imagination of readers worldwide."},{"sourceSlug":"charles-darwin","sourceName":"Charles Darwin","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"evolutionary-theory-of-evolution","targetName":"Theory of Evolution","context":"Darwin's groundbreaking theory that influenced Verne's vision of global connectivity and technological innovation."},{"sourceSlug":"john-wesley-powell","sourceName":"John Wesley Powell","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"phileas-fogg","targetName":"Phileas Fogg","context":"Powell's adventurous spirit and exploration of the American West influenced Verne's creation of Phileas Fogg."}],"places":[{"name":"London, England","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Meeting Point"},{"name":"New York City, USA","role":"Destination"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Around the World in Eighty Days","type":"Reference article","slug":"around-the-world-in-eighty-days"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1219561","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Around_the_World_in_Eighty_Days"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Around the World in Eighty Days left a notable mark on Modern history, documented across 78 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Around the World in Eighty Days (French: Le Tour du monde en quatre-vingts jours) is an adventure novel by Jules Verne (1828–1905), first published as a serial in Le Temps (6 November–22 December 1872), then in book form by Pierre-Jules Hetzel (1873), and one of the most popular and widely translated French novels of the 19th century. The novel follows the meticulous, punctual English gentleman Phileas Fogg — member of the Reform Club in London, a man of clockwork habits — who bets £20,000 (half his fortune) that he can circumnavigate the globe in eighty days, a feat that the advances of steam travel and the recent opening of the Suez Canal (1869) and the completion of the transcontinental US railroad (1869) have made theoretically possible. Accompanied by his newly hired French manservant Passepartout, Fogg travels from London through the Suez Canal to India (by train across the subcontinent), then to Hong Kong, Yokohama, San Francisco (by steamer), across North America (by train, encountering a snowstorm, a Sioux attack on the train, and a missing bridge), back to New York and across the Atlantic to Liverpool — and then believes he has missed his deadline by a day, only to discover that by travelling eastward he has gained a day, arriving at the Reform Club precisely on time.

Around the World in Eighty Days is one of the founding texts of the adventure novel as a genre — its combination of geographical suspense, technological modernity (steam trains, steamships, the telegraph), and the puzzle-plot of the wager created the template for the adventure story in which the entire world is the stage. The novel reflects the Victorian globalisation of the 1870s — the steamship and railway networks that connected the British Empire, the opening of the Suez Canal, and the completion of the US transcontinental railroad — presenting a world in which the telegraph and steam power have, for the first time, made the circumnavigation of the globe a matter of timetables rather than years of sailing.

Around the World in Eighty Days has been adapted into films at least seven times (the most famous being the 1956 Michael Anderson film starring David Niven as Fogg) and has entered the cultural imagination as the archetypal adventure of speed, global reach, and the conquest of distance by technology.","subjects":["French Literature","Modern Era","Jules Verne","Adventure Novel","Victorian Literature","Globalisation","Technology","19th Century"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Published in 1872, Around the World in Eighty Days remains a seminal work of science fiction that continues to captivate readers with its vivid descriptions of exotic locales and technological innovations.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
